Krita Nova 39 - text layers, like Photoshop (text tool, part 4)

Each new text gets a vector layer of its own, like a Photoshop type layer:

- The layer is added above the current layer, in the same group, and becomes
  the current layer. While nothing is typed it is called "Text".
- When editing the text ends (click away, Esc, Ctrl+Enter, another text), the
  layer is named after its text (up to 40 characters), undoably - again after
  later edits, until the user renames the layer.
- A text that was never typed into is removed together with its layer
  (patch 38).
- Text in a shape stays in the layer of its shape, and text created in a
  vector selection (selection mask) stays there, as before.

SvgCreateTextStrategy::createTextLayer() makes the layer (as part of the
"create text" undo step); the layers carry the node property
krita-nova-text-layer-name (the name the tool gave them).
SvgTextTool::updateTextLayerName() renames, removeIfEmpty() removes the layer
of an empty text.
